WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.660
How do you count chromosomes and spot swapped pieces inside a crowded nucleus?

2
00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:09.640
Chromosomes live there in pairs, one homolog from each parent.

3
00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:14.060
Karyotyping uses stained spreads to count and match them.

4
00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:20.560
Fluorescent DNA probes that bind matching sequences, a method called FISH, make

5
00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:22.020
chosen chromosomes glow.

6
00:00:23.133 --> 00:00:28.653
First, catch a dividing cell in mitosis, when each chromosome packs into a compact

7
00:00:28.653 --> 00:00:33.833
rod. Spreading and staining those rods lets you count every chromosome.

8
00:00:35.273 --> 00:00:37.333
In people, the usual count is 46.

9
00:00:38.813 --> 00:00:40.773
The stain leaves stripes, called bands.

10
00:00:41.733 --> 00:00:46.913
Sorting rods by size and banding matches homologous pairs, forming a karyotype.

11
00:00:48.033 --> 00:00:53.193
To track one chromosome, add fluorescent DNA probes that stick to its matching

12
00:00:53.193 --> 00:00:58.453
sequence. Labelling whole chromosomes in colour is chromosome painting.

13
00:00:59.813 --> 00:01:05.593
After mitosis, each chromosome partly unpacks, yet keeps its own space, a chromosome

14
00:01:05.593 --> 00:01:11.633
territory. Stained spreads and fluorescent probes turn a nucleus into countable

15
00:01:11.633 --> 00:01:14.813
pairs, traceable pieces, and mapped territories.

16
00:01:16.017 --> 00:01:19.237
Now test what a chromosome count alone can tell you.

17
00:01:20.277 --> 00:01:24.357
Suppose a stained spread shows 46 chromosomes, the usual count.

18
00:01:25.737 --> 00:01:31.037
Does that normal count rule out extra chromosome 21 DNA, or a disrupted gene?

19
00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:38.730
No, count is only the first clue.

20
00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:46.530
A cell with 46 chromosomes can still hold extra chromosome 21 material, attached to

21
00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:47.530
another chromosome.

22
00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:53.830
A swap, called a translocation, can place a gene beside another gene's strong

23
00:01:53.830 --> 00:01:56.670
control region without changing total DNA.

24
00:01:58.010 --> 00:02:03.350
And a tumor's two copies of chromosome 13 can both carry one mutant gene version,

25
00:02:03.710 --> 00:02:05.170
leaving no normal copy.

26
00:02:06.283 --> 00:02:10.463
So experimenters do not judge by count or chromosome size alone.

27
00:02:11.583 --> 00:02:16.823
They check what DNA was gained or lost, trace breaks with probes, and test which

28
00:02:16.823 --> 00:02:18.063
gene versions remain.

29
00:02:19.563 --> 00:02:21.483
Chromosome painting has a limit too.

30
00:02:22.823 --> 00:02:26.983
Painted territories press together like yarn, with loose loops poking into

31
00:02:26.983 --> 00:02:32.743
neighbours. Whole-nucleus painting maps those territories, not whether edges mix.

32
00:02:33.867 --> 00:02:36.807
These methods unlocked three landmark discoveries.

33
00:02:38.207 --> 00:02:44.707
In 1959, Jérôme Lejeune, Marthe Gautier, and Raymond Turpin counted chromosomes in

34
00:02:44.707 --> 00:02:46.407
nine children with Down syndrome.

35
00:02:47.707 --> 00:02:51.527
Their cultured cells held 47 chromosomes, not 46.

36
00:02:52.867 --> 00:02:58.467
That extra chromosome, later numbered 21, tied chromosome content to the condition.

37
00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:06.130
In 1960, Peter Nowell and David Hungerford saw a small chromosome in chronic myeloid

38
00:03:06.130 --> 00:03:07.410
leukaemia blood cells.

39
00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:15.050
In 1973, Janet Rowley showed it was chromosome 22, its missing piece attached to

40
00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:21.510
chromosome 9. Later work tied that join to the fusion of two genes, BCR and ABL.

41
00:03:22.383 --> 00:03:28.863
In 1963, James Till, Ernest McCulloch, and Andrew Becker tracked marrow cells

42
00:03:28.863 --> 00:03:30.763
injected into irradiated mice.

43
00:03:32.303 --> 00:03:36.883
Chromosome markers showed each spleen colony was a clone, grown from one cell.

44
00:03:38.223 --> 00:03:43.043
Counting, banding and painting chromosomes make number, rearrangements and lineage

45
00:03:43.043 --> 00:03:43.383
visible.
